// Target Dialect — target-specific (x64) machine instructions, the lowest IR
// tier before machine-code emission. The Std→Target lowering (StdToX64Conversion)
// emits these ops; the register allocator colors their virtual registers into
// physical GPRs.
//
// THIN for maxon-shv2 M1: carries exactly the x64 ops the `return 42` slice
// needs — materialize a constant into a register (movRegImm / movRegImm32),
// move a value between registers (movRegReg, for the R8 return move + regalloc
// copies), the frame/return control ops (prologue / epilogue / ret), and the
// three ops the runtime entry stub `mrt_start` is built from: a direct call to
// the user entry (callDirect), the custom-ABI→Win64 bridge move (movReg32Reg32,
// `mov ecx, r8d`), and an IAT-relative indirect call (iatCall, `call ExitProcess`
// through the import table). Later milestones GROW `TargetOp` with the ALU /
// compare-branch / general call / memory / FP (XMM) ops as features land, and
// GROW the register representation with the XMM register file. arm64 support
// arrives at its own milestone; M1 is x64-windows only, so `TargetModule`
// currently carries only x64 ops.
//
// v1 returns the primary value in R8 (NOT RAX) under its self-designed calling
// convention; the physical GPR enum here includes R8 so B2's regalloc + return
// move can target it. v1's per-variant `X64OpMeta` scheduling/regalloc metadata
// is preserved in thinned form (`TargetOpMeta`) so a new op cannot be added
// without declaring its memory/store/call/flags semantics; B2 GROWS it with the
// register-allocator `OpPattern` + implicit-register masks when it ports the
// allocator (deferred here because they drag in the whole regalloc scaffold).

// Every physical register the allocator colors into: the 16 general-purpose
// registers of x86-64 in encoding order (0=rax .. 15=r15), then the 16 SSE
// registers (16=xmm0 .. 31=xmm15). The return value lands in R8 per Maxon's
// internal calling convention.
//
// ⭐ ONE ENUM, TWO CLASSES — not two enums. A register's CLASS (`RegClass`) is a
// property of the register, so it is derived from the rawValue here and written
// down nowhere else. v1 mints a SECOND enum for its FP file and pays for it in
// every structure that names a register: a parallel spill op, a parallel reload,
// a parallel move, a parallel mask type. The allocator's machinery — `RegMask`,
// the forbidden mask, the spill slot, the Hall witness — is all class-agnostic
// bit work that does not care WHICH file a bit names, and one enum is what lets
// it stay that way.
//
// ⚠ The low 4 bits of an XMM's rawValue are its ENCODING number (xmm3 = 19 =
// 0b10011 → reg field 3), so `rawValue and 0xF` encodes and bit 4 classifies.
// That is deliberate: it is why an emitter can form a ModRM reg field without a
// lookup table, exactly as `X64CondCode`'s backing value IS the jcc nibble.
export enum X64Register
	rax
	rcx
	rdx
	rbx
	rsp
	rbp
	rsi
	rdi
	r8
	r9
	r10
	r11
	r12
	r13
	r14
	r15
	xmm0 = 16
	xmm1
	xmm2
	xmm3
	xmm4
	xmm5
	xmm6
	xmm7
	xmm8
	xmm9
	xmm10
	xmm11
	xmm12
	xmm13
	xmm14
	xmm15
end 'X64Register'

// Which register FILE a value lives in. A value's class is what the value IS —
// intrinsic and permanent, fixed by its type at birth: an f64 lives in an XMM and
// an i64 lives in a GPR, and no amount of spilling, splitting, or recoloring
// changes that.
//
// ⭐⭐ THE CLASS IS NOT A FORBIDDEN MASK, AND THIS IS THE RUNG'S CENTRAL
// DISTINCTION. It is tempting to spend no new column and encode "not a GPR" as
// 16 set bits in a value's `forbidden` mask. That is WRONG, and it fails
// silently — `LivenessResult.clearForbidden` ZEROES that mask, and
// `SplitLiveRanges` calls it on every spill victim and every fresh id, so the
// class would be wiped mid-allocation and the float would be colored into a GPR.
// And the wipe is CORRECT and must stay: `forbidden` is what the ops a value
// crosses TOOK AWAY — contextual, and recomputable precisely BECAUSE a split cuts
// the live range that earned it. Two facts with two different lifetimes cannot
// share one column.
// `regClassOf` — the ONE place the GPR/XMM boundary is decided — lives in RegBits
// beside the pool masks, because it is keyed on `RegNum` and RegBits is what owns
// that type. This enum stays here: it names a dialect concept and depends on
// nothing.
export enum RegClass
	gpr
	xmm
end 'RegClass'

// Every value's register class, indexed by ValueId — the allocator's answer to "which
// file does this value live in?", and the thing that keeps an f64 out of a GPR.
//
// ⭐⭐ IT IS PRODUCED BY THE LOWERING, NOT DERIVED IN THE BACKEND, and that direction is
// the whole design. A value's class is fixed by its Std TYPE (`stdTypeIsFloat`), and
// `StdToX64Conversion` is the last pass that can see a type at all — the Target tier
// carries none. `lowerConst`'s own comment has said so since M3: *"The op's own StdType
// picks the register file (GP vs XMM)"*. So the lowering writes this column at the moment
// it mints each virtual register, and Std ValueIds pass through unchanged, which is what
// lets one dense array serve both tiers.
//
// ⚠ THE ALTERNATIVE — recovering the class in the allocator by walking the ops — LOOKS
// cheaper and is the trap. It cannot work locally: `movRegReg`, `loadRegSlot` and
// `xchgRegReg` are CLASS-AGNOSTIC by design (that is what one enum buys), so a copy's
// class is only knowable from its source, and a reload's only from whatever was spilled.
// Recovering it means a propagation pass that reconstructs, approximately and late,
// exactly what the Std type stated exactly and early. That is a second type system in the
// tier furthest from the source — the same argument `StdOp.binOp.operandType` is built on,
// and it has the same answer: state the fact once, where it is known.
//
// ⚠ A FRESH ID MINTED BY A SPLIT INHERITS ITS VICTIM'S CLASS. A split cuts a live range;
// it does not change what the value IS. This is the one place a value's class is set
// after lowering, and it is a copy, never a re-derivation.
export typealias ValueClassColumn = Array with RegClass

// A machine register operand: either a concrete physical register — named by its
// NEUTRAL `RegNum`, not a concrete ISA enum — or a virtual register identified by
// the SSA ValueId that defined it. Every `movImm` result arrives as a `virtual`;
// the register allocator rewrites each into a `physical` during coloring.
//
// ⭐ THE PHYSICAL PAYLOAD IS A `RegNum`, NOT AN `X64Register`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
// NEUTRALIZATION. One allocator serves both ISAs (USER RULING 2026-07-17), so the
// ops it produces and rewrites — `movRegReg`, the edge copies, the spill/reload —
// must name registers in a number the allocator understands and NEITHER encoder is
// bound to. x64's `RegNum`s ARE its `X64Register.rawValue`s (0..31); arm64's are its
// `Arm64Register.rawValue`s (x0..x30 = 0..30, d0..d31 = 31..62). The colorer works
// in `RegNum`, and each ENCODER maps a resolved `RegNum` back to its own file
// (`X64Backend.resolvePhysical` / the arm64 encoder). This is why an `X64Register`
// no longer appears on a lowered op: it belongs to the encoder, one tier below.
export union TargetVReg
	physical(reg RegNum)
	virtual(id ValueId)
end 'TargetVReg'

// A physical x64 operand, from an `X64Register`. The x64 lowering + entry stub name their fixed
// registers as `X64Register`s (rax, rcx, …); this maps one to its neutral `RegNum` — the number the
// unified vreg carries — in ONE place.
//
// ⚠ The `.rawValue` is bound to a local FIRST, deliberately: the bootstrap's union-case argument
// type-checker cannot lower an `EnumMember.rawValue` expression written DIRECTLY as a case argument
// (`TargetVReg.physical(X64Register.rax.rawValue)` → E9001, "Enum kinds require lookup via type
// registry"). Binding it to `n` presents the constructor a plain integer, which is also why every
// x64 physical-vreg site routes through here rather than spelling the cast inline.
export function x64PhysReg(reg X64Register) returns TargetVReg
	let n = reg.rawValue
	return TargetVReg.physical(n)
end 'x64PhysReg'

// x64 condition codes — the `tttn` nibble of a `jcc`/`setcc` opcode. The backing
// value IS the nibble, so the `jcc` encoder forms its opcode as `0x80 | rawValue`
// (e.g. `equal` = 0x4 → `0F 84` = JE) with no separate lookup table.
//
// ⭐ THE FAMILY IS CHOSEN BY THE COMPARE'S OPERAND TYPE, NOT BY ITS PREDICATE.
// `cmp` on two i64s is a subtraction and answers in OF/SF, which the SIGNED JL/JG
// family reads. `ucomisd` on two f64s leaves OF=SF=AF=0 always and answers in
// ZF/PF/CF — the same three flags an UNSIGNED compare writes — so a float compare
// branches with the UNSIGNED JB/JA family, and lowering one to `jl` would read
// flags `ucomisd` never wrote. `StdCmpPred.less` alone therefore does NOT
// determine the condition code and never did; it only looked that way while every
// Std compare was a signed i64. `condCodeForPred` takes the operand type.
//
// ⚠ `parity`/`noParity` exist for the FOURTH answer a float compare has and an
// integer compare does not: UNORDERED, the result when either operand is NaN.
// `ucomisd` reports it as ZF=PF=CF=1 — the same flags as "equal" but for PF — so
// PF is the only bit that distinguishes NaN from equality, and IEEE-754 requires
// every relational predicate be FALSE on unordered. See specs-shv2/float-compare-branch.md.
export enum X64CondCode
	below = 0x2  // JB  (unsigned <,  float <)
	aboveEqual = 0x3  // JAE (unsigned >=, float >=)
	equal = 0x4  // JE  / JZ
	notEqual = 0x5  // JNE / JNZ
	belowEqual = 0x6  // JBE (unsigned <=, float <=)
	above = 0x7  // JA  (unsigned >,  float >)
	parity = 0xA  // JP  (PF=1 — the float compare's UNORDERED/NaN edge)
	noParity = 0xB  // JNP (PF=0 — ORDERED: neither operand is NaN)
	less = 0xC  // JL  (signed <)
	greaterEqual = 0xD  // JGE (signed >=)
	lessEqual = 0xE  // JLE (signed <=)
	greater = 0xF  // JG  (signed >)
end 'X64CondCode'

// Every arm64 physical register, its rawValue the NEUTRAL `RegNum` the allocator colors into: the
// 31 general-purpose registers x0..x30 (0..30), then the 32 SIMD/FP registers d0..d31 (31..62).
// One enum, two classes, exactly as `X64Register` — a register's CLASS is its number's position
// relative to the file's `firstVector` (31 for arm64), written down nowhere else.
//
// ⚠ SP and XZR are NOT in the allocatable `RegNum` space. In arm64's encoding they SHARE the GPR
// field value 31, which in the unified `RegNum` numbering is already d0 — so they cannot take a
// `RegNum`. They are declared ABOVE the register file (64/65) as encoder-only names: the allocator
// never produces them, `fromRawValue` on a colored `RegNum` (0..62) never yields them, and the
// arm64 encoder references them by name for the stack pointer / zero register. 63 is the "no
// register" sentinel (`RegisterFileSize`) and deliberately names no register here.
export enum Arm64Register
	x0
	x1
	x2
	x3
	x4
	x5
	x6
	x7
	x8
	x9
	x10
	x11
	x12
	x13
	x14
	x15
	x16
	x17
	x18
	x19
	x20
	x21
	x22
	x23
	x24
	x25
	x26
	x27
	x28
	x29
	x30
	d0 = 31
	d1
	d2
	d3
	d4
	d5
	d6
	d7
	d8
	d9
	d10
	d11
	d12
	d13
	d14
	d15
	d16
	d17
	d18
	d19
	d20
	d21
	d22
	d23
	d24
	d25
	d26
	d27
	d28
	d29
	d30
	d31
	sp = 64
	xzr = 65
end 'Arm64Register'

// arm64 condition codes — the 4-bit machine condition field a `b.cond` / `cset` carries, so an
// encoder forms the field straight from `rawValue`. The scalar core uses the signed int family
// (`lt`/`le`/`gt`/`ge`), the unsigned family (`hi`/`ls`), equality (`eq`/`ne`), and — for IEEE-754
// float `<` / `<=` — the N-flag conditions `mi`/`pl`.
//
// ⚠ THE NaN-SAFE FLOAT MAPPING. `fcmp` sets N=0 on an unordered (NaN) result, so float `<` lowers
// to `mi` (N==1, FALSE on NaN) and float `<=` to `ls` (C==0 ∨ Z==1, also FALSE on NaN) — NOT `lt`
// / `le`, which would read TRUE on NaN and violate IEEE-754's "every relational predicate is false
// on unordered". This mirrors the x64 float compare's use of the UNSIGNED family (see X64CondCode).
export enum Arm64CondCode
	eq = 0  // Z==1                 (== ; float ==)
	ne = 1  // Z==0                 (!= ; float !=)
	mi = 4  // N==1                 (float <  — false on NaN)
	pl = 5  // N==0                 (float >= — the ordered complement)
	hi = 8  // C==1 ∧ Z==0          (unsigned > ; float >)
	ls = 9  // C==0 ∨ Z==1          (unsigned <= ; float <=)
	ge = 10  // N==V                (signed >=)
	lt = 11  // N!=V                (signed <)
	gt = 12  // Z==0 ∧ N==V         (signed >)
	le = 13  // Z==1 ∨ N!=V         (signed <=)
end 'Arm64CondCode'

// A set of physical registers: bit `i` is set iff `X64Register` number `i` (its
// `rawValue`) is in the set — so bits 0-15 name GPRs and bits 16-31 name XMMs, and
// a mask spans BOTH classes. Used for an op's implicit register reads/writes — the
// registers it touches WITHOUT naming them as an explicit operand. Picking /
// testing a register is a single bit op, no set iteration. A full machine word so
// bit-complement (`not pool`) never overflows the range.
//
// ⚠ A RegMask is CLASS-AGNOSTIC: it is a bag of bits and it does not know which
// file they name. That is what lets one mask type serve both classes — and it is
// exactly why every PREDICATE over a mask ("is this value confined to that set?",
// "is the pool full?") must be asked PER CLASS, against a class-filtered mask.
// A cross-class mask compared against a whole-file `fullRegisterMask()` answers a
// question nobody asked.
export typealias RegMask = int(0 to u64.max)

// A spill-slot index: the k-th 8-byte slot the cold-spill live-range splitter
// (SplitLiveRanges) reserves in a function's frame. It is a dense per-function
// index — slot 0, 1, 2, … — NOT a byte displacement; the x64 encoder turns it into
// a `[rsp + disp]` displacement (`spillSlotDisp`) at emit time, so the frame layout
// (shadow space, alignment) lives in one place. Each spilled VALUE gets its own
// slot, and exactly one: its store is anchored at its def, which dominates every
// reload, so a value relieved at a SECOND peak reloads out of the slot it already
// has rather than being written a second time. (No slot coalescing ACROSS values
// yet — a future optimization.)
export typealias SpillSlot = int(0 to u64.max)

export typealias SpillSlotArray = Array with SpillSlot

// The immediate shift count of an `shl`/`sar` by a constant — the counts the INSTRUCTION takes as
// written, on which its imm8 form and its `cl` form provably agree. Typed this narrowly on purpose:
// the cast at the lowering boundary then PROVES the fold's range promise (see
// `constFitsBinOpImmediate`) rather than silently truncating a count that broke it.
//
// ⚠ THE `0 to 63` HERE IS THE ONE FORCED RESTATEMENT of the bound, whose CANONICAL declaration is
// TypeRules' `MinShiftCount`/`MaxUnguardedShiftCount` — read ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE there for what a
// count outside it MEANS (it is legal, and it is not this shift). It is spelled out again only
// because a ranged typealias's bounds must be literals or builtin sized-type bounds: the language
// has no way to write `int(MinShiftCount to MaxUnguardedShiftCount)`. Nothing else in the compiler
// may restate it, and these two numbers must move with the ones over there.
export typealias ShiftCount = int(0 to 63)

// Per-variant static metadata backing for TargetOp (read via `op.rawValue`),
// consumed by the instruction scheduler + register allocator. The scheduling
// flags plus the implicit-GPR read/def masks the register allocator's liveness
// consumes — same "you cannot add an op without declaring its facts" discipline
// `StdOpMeta` enforces.
export type TargetOpMeta
	// Accesses memory / has a side effect the scheduler must order as a barrier.
	export let isMemory as bool
	// Writes memory / has an externally-observable side effect (pure loads are
	// memory but not stores).
	export let isStore as bool
	// Full call-barrier semantics (clobbers caller-saved registers).
	export let isCall as bool
	// ⚠ THERE IS NO `setsFlags` HERE, AND PUTTING ONE BACK WOULD BE A MISTAKE. It existed
	// until 2026-07-14: declared on all 40 variants, READ BY NOTHING — and, worse, it
	// CONTRADICTED the live answer. `StdOp.const` claimed `clobbersFlags: true` while
	// `movRegImm32` here said `setsFlags: false`. Two descriptions of ONE hardware fact,
	// drifted apart, so the DEAD one was quietly right and the LIVE one quietly wrong — at a
	// cost of 3 instructions on every `if a > 0 and b > 0`.
	//
	// It was a PORT ARTIFACT. v1 genuinely reads `setsFlags` off a target op
	// (MirToX64Conversion.maxon:409), because v1 fuses compare/branch at the MIR→X64
	// boundary, where the target op already exists. shv2 moved that scan UP to the Std tier
	// — `recordFusableCompare` runs on StdOps, BEFORE lowering, because its answer is what
	// DECIDES what the lowering emits — and then kept v1's field anyway. Two homes for one
	// fact, one of them unreachable.
	//
	// ⇒ The flags fact lives ONCE, at the Std tier, as `StdOpMeta.clobbersFlags`. If a
	// TARGET-tier pass ever genuinely needs per-instruction flag behaviour (a peephole, an
	// instruction scheduler), add it back THEN, WITH its reader. Never speculatively: this
	// field was written 40 times and read 0, and that is precisely how it got to be wrong
	// without anyone noticing.
	//
	// Registers this op READS without naming them as an explicit operand: R8 for
	// `ret` today; the volatile set a `call` reads/clobbers grows in when M5
	// colors calls. Liveness folds these into `forbiddenPhys`.
	export let implicitUses as RegMask
	// Registers this op WRITES without an explicit operand — a `call`'s
	// caller-saved clobber set (M5). 0 for every op the M5.1 spine colors, so a
	// value live across such an op must avoid these registers.
	export let implicitDefs as RegMask
end 'TargetOpMeta'

export union TargetOp
	// Frame setup: reserve `stackSize` bytes of stack (`sub rsp, stackSize`).
	// shv2 M1 uses a frame-pointer-omitted (rsp-only) frame — no `push rbp` — so
	// the entry stub's `sub rsp, 0x28` shadow-space reservation IS a `prologue`.
	// Writes EFLAGS (it is a `sub`). Callee-saved pushes GROW in when the register
	// allocator first promotes into callee-saved regs (none at M1). Inserted by B2's
	// prologue/epilogue pass at a function's entry block.
	prologue(stackSize StackSize) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Frame teardown (`add rsp, stackSize`), the exact mirror of `prologue`.
	// Placed just before `ret` on a return block. Carries its own `stackSize` so
	// the encoder is self-contained (no frame tracker threaded through emit).
	epilogue(stackSize StackSize) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// `ret` instruction — return control to the caller.
	// implicitUses = 0x100 = the R8 bit (`X64Register.r8.rawValue` == 8, so 1<<8):
	// `ret` reads the primary-return register. This is what lets the register
	// allocator ADMIT R8 into the pool safely — forbiddenPhys proves nothing is live
	// across the `mov r8, retval` return move. (A union-backing literal cannot
	// reference a named `let`, so the mask is written inline.)
	ret = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0x100, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Unconditional near jump to a block (`jmp rel32`, 0xE9). `targetBlockId` names a
	// function-local block; emitFunctionChunk resolves it to a chunk-local rel32 (a
	// function's blocks are contiguous, so intra-function jumps never cross a chunk
	// boundary). Reads/sets no flags. Placed in the control band next to `ret`.
	jmp(targetBlockId BlockId) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Conditional near jump (`jcc rel32`, 0F 80+cc). Taken to `targetBlockId` when the
	// EFLAGS the preceding `cmpRegReg` set satisfy `cond`, else falls through. Writes NO
	// flags — `jcc` READS them.
	jcc(cond X64CondCode, targetBlockId BlockId) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Materialize a 64-bit immediate into `dest` (`mov reg, imm64`).
	movRegImm(dest TargetVReg, value MachineWord) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Materialize a 32-bit immediate into `dest` (`mov reg32, imm32`, which
	// zero-extends to the full 64-bit register). B2 picks this over movRegImm
	// for values that fit 32 bits (e.g. the literal 42) — the encoding is shorter.
	movRegImm32(dest TargetVReg, value MachineWord) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit register-to-register move (`mov dest, src`). B2 emits this for the
	// primary-return move (value → R8) and for register-allocation copies.
	movRegReg(destReg TargetVReg, srcReg TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 32-bit register-to-register move (`mov dest32, src32`, zero-extending to
	// 64 bits). B2 emits exactly one at M1: the custom-ABI→Win64 bridge in
	// `mrt_start` (`mov ecx, r8d`) that hands main's R8 return value to
	// ExitProcess's ECX argument slot.
	movReg32Reg32(destReg TargetVReg, srcReg TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit register-to-register subtract (`sub dest, rhs`, i.e. `dest = lhs - rhs`).
	// GENUINELY two-address on x64 (SUB has no 3-operand form), so the operand model
	// declares `dest` a REUSE-of-`lhs` def: the allocator gives `dest` `lhs`'s register
	// when `lhs` dies here (no copy — the common case), else materializes `mov dest, lhs`
	// before the op. The encoder emits only `sub dest, rhs` (dest already holds lhs). The
	// three explicit operands `(dest, lhs, rhs)` let liveness see that `lhs` is read here
	// even when it also outlives — the old two-field `subRegReg(destReg, srcReg)` hid the
	// pre-emitted seed `mov` and assumed distinct registers, false under reuse.
	// SUB writes EFLAGS. ALU band; appended before `cmpRegReg`.
	subRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit register-to-register signed multiply (`imul dest, rhs`, i.e.
	// `dest = lhs * rhs`). Two-operand form `imul r64, r/m64` (0F AF /r), genuinely
	// two-address, so like `subRegReg` its `dest` is a REUSE-of-`lhs` def. The encoder
	// emits `imul dest, rhs` (dest already holds lhs; its reg field is the DESTINATION —
	// see X64Backend.encodeImulRegReg). IMUL writes OF/CF.
	imulRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit register negate (`neg dest`, i.e. `dest = -src`). Group-3 opcode-extension
	// form (`F7 /3`), genuinely two-address (one in-place operand), so `dest` is a
	// REUSE-of-`src` def. The encoder emits `neg dest` (dest already holds src).
	// NEG writes EFLAGS.
	negReg(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit register-to-register compare (`cmp lhs, rhs`, 0x39, i.e. `lhs - rhs`
	// discarding the result, keeping the flags). The `r/m, r` shape shared with
	// `mov`/`sub` (reg field = the SUBTRAHEND `rhsReg`, r/m field = `lhsReg`), so
	// after it a signed `jcc` tests `lhs <cond> rhs`. CMP writes EFLAGS.
	cmpRegReg(lhsReg TargetVReg, rhsReg TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit address ADD via `lea dest, [base + index]` (REX.W 8D /r + SIB, scale 1).
	// The 3-operand form of `+`: `dest`, `base`, `index` are THREE independent operands
	// (NO reuse), so `+` needs no seed copy at all and — because `lea` computes an
	// address rather than an arithmetic result — writes NO flags. That is ALSO why
	// `StdOp.binOp`'s `clobbersFlags: true` is a deliberate over-approximation: `add`
	// lands here and clobbers nothing, but `sub`/`mul` do, and per-variant metadata
	// cannot see the opcode (see StdOpMeta.clobbersFlags).
	// This is what makes `sum = sum + i` in a loop a single copy-free instruction. SIB
	// base low-3-bits 101 (rbp/r13) and index == rsp are the encoding traps the backend
	// asserts/handles (see X64Backend.encodeLeaRegRegReg). ALU band, appended after
	// `cmpRegReg`.
	leaRegRegReg(dest TargetVReg, base TargetVReg, index TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit `a + imm` / `a - imm` via `lea dest, [base + disp32]` (REX.W 8D /r, signed
	// disp; `a - imm` uses a negative displacement). `dest` and `base` are independent
	// (NO reuse), and the immediate needs NO register. Sets no flags, like every `lea`.
	// Produced from a `binOpImm(add|sub, ...)`. Appended after `leaRegRegReg`.
	leaRegRegImm32(dest TargetVReg, base TargetVReg, disp Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit `a * imm` via `imul dest, src, imm32` (REX.W 69 /r id) — the THREE-operand
	// signed multiply: `dest` and `src` are independent (NO reuse), the multiplier is an
	// immediate needing no register. IMUL writes OF/CF. Produced from a
	// `binOpImm(mul, ...)`. Appended after `leaRegRegImm32`.
	imulRegRegImm32(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit compare against an immediate (`cmp reg, imm32`, REX.W 81 /7 id — the sign-
	// extended group-1 form). The literal occupies no register; a following signed `jcc`
	// tests `reg <cond> imm`. CMP writes EFLAGS. Produced from a
	// `cmpImm(...)`. Appended after `imulRegRegImm32`, at the END of the ALU band.
	cmpRegImm32(reg TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Exchange the contents of two physical registers (`xchg a, b`, REX.W 87 /r).
	// POST-COLORING and physical-only: the register allocator's SSA-destruction step
	// emits it to break a parallel-copy CYCLE on a CFG edge with no scratch register
	// (regalloc2's trick). It has TWO defs, so it would violate the single-def SSA
	// rule the colorer relies on — legal precisely because the allocator never sees
	// it (it is spliced in AFTER coloring, so it is invisible to liveness/coloring).
	// `xchg` does NOT set flags. Appended at the END of the reg-operand ALU band.
	xchgRegReg(a TargetVReg, b TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Direct near call to a named function (`call rel32`). The concat step
	// resolves `targetName` to the callee's `.text` placement as a rel32
	// displacement. `mrt_start` uses this to `call main`. Full call-barrier
	// semantics (clobbers caller-saved registers).
	//
	// implicitDefs = 0xFFFF0FC7 = every CALLER-SAVED register of BOTH files that the custom
	// ABI clobbers across a call.
	//
	// The GPR half is 0xFC7: {rax=0, rcx=1, rdx=2, rsi=6, rdi=7, r8=8, r9=9, r10=10, r11=11}
	// — bits 0x1|0x2|0x4|0x40|0x80|0x100|0x200|0x400|0x800. rbx (0x8) and r12–r15 (0xF000)
	// are CALLEE-saved and DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED (rbx being wrongly clobbered was a shipped
	// v1 regalloc bug — project_regalloc_crosscall_spill_fixes).
	//
	// The XMM half is 0xFFFF0000: ALL SIXTEEN of xmm0–15 (bits 16–31), because in Wave 1
	// every XMM is caller-saved. That is bought deliberately — a float live across a call is
	// then forbidden its ENTIRE file, has no register left, and FORCE-SPILLS. v1 got this
	// wrong in both directions and its own float tests are disabled because of it: its
	// `createX64CallerSavedFps()` takes no `os` parameter while its GPR twin does, so it
	// applies a SysV table to Win64 and declares xmm6/7 caller-saved when Win64 preserves
	// them; and its FP callee-saved half is literally empty.
	//
	// ⚠ TAKING xmm6–15 AS CALLEE-SAVED IS WAVE 2, NOT A TWEAK. Win64 preserves all 128 bits
	// of them, and `movsd` ZEROES bits 64–127 — so honouring that needs 16-byte spill slots
	// and drags in leaf frame misalignment at the same time.
	//
	// The liveness pass folds this mask into `forbiddenPhys` for every value live ACROSS a
	// call, so such a value is forced into a callee-saved register OF ITS OWN FILE — and
	// `assertCallClobberConsistent` guards the mask against drifting apart from the pool it
	// is subtracted from. That assert is why this literal and `callerSavedMask()` MUST move
	// in one commit: it is the one-fact-one-place guard doing its job.
	// A union-backing literal cannot reference a named `let`, so the mask is inline.
	callDirect(targetName ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0xFFFF0FC7}
	// Indirect call through an Import Address Table slot (`call [rip+disp32]`).
	// `slotIndex` names the IAT entry the PE writer patches to. `mrt_start` uses
	// this to `call ExitProcess`; `lowerOsAllocPages` uses it to `call VirtualAlloc`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE SAME CLOBBER MASK AS `callDirect`, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.** This field read 0
	// until P1.0r while the line above it said "full call-barrier semantics" — the sentence and
	// the mask were one fact written twice, and they disagreed. It was INERT only because the
	// single `iatCall` in the tree was `mrt_start`'s `ExitProcess`, a hand-built stub with no
	// virtual register live across anything. `osAllocPages` put an `iatCall` inside a REGISTER-
	// ALLOCATED function (`__slab_alloc`'s grow block) and the zero became a miscompile the same
	// day: `size` coloured RAX and `total` coloured R10, both clobbered by `VirtualAlloc`, so
	// `__slab_cursor` was published as `base + base` and the bump path was never taken again —
	// 64 KiB committed per allocation, measured at 133 MB for 2,000 Points.
	//
	// The mask is the CUSTOM ABI's caller-saved set, not Win64's, and that is deliberate rather
	// than sloppy: Win64's volatile GPRs {rax, rcx, rdx, r8–r11} are a strict SUBSET of the custom
	// ABI's {rax, rcx, rdx, rsi, rdi, r8–r11} (Win64 PRESERVES rsi/rdi), so this over-forbids by
	// exactly two registers and can never under-forbid. A second, Win64-exact mask would be a
	// third copy of "which registers a call destroys" — the very shape that caused this bug — and
	// it would buy two registers at a call site that has five callee-saved ones spare.
	// `assertCallClobberConsistent` asserts BOTH call ops against `callerSavedMask()`, so the
	// three cannot drift. A union-backing literal cannot reference a named `let`, which is why the
	// mask is spelled inline here and guarded there rather than shared.
	iatCall(slotIndex RelocSlotIndex) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0xFFFF0FC7}
	// Sign-extend RAX into RDX:RAX (`cqo`, REX.W 99) so the following `idiv` divides the
	// full 128-bit RDX:RAX by its operand. Both registers are IMPLICIT (no explicit
	// operand): implicitUses = 0x1 (the RAX bit — `X64Register.rax.rawValue` == 0, so
	// 1<<0 — it READS RAX), implicitDefs = 0x4 (the RDX bit — `rdx.rawValue` == 2, so
	// 1<<2 — it WRITES RDX). Liveness folds implicitDefs into `forbiddenPhys` (a value
	// live across `cqo` must avoid RDX), and implicitUses keeps RAX — which the div
	// lowering moved the dividend into — reserved across it. `cqo` leaves EFLAGS
	// untouched. (A union-backing literal cannot reference a named `let`, so the masks
	// are written inline.) APPENDED at the END of the union (after the call band) so no
	// existing `… to iatCall` range arm can SILENTLY swallow it — each such arm instead
	// fails to compile until it is consciously extended (the band-append invariant).
	cqo = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0x1, implicitDefs: 0x4}
	// Signed divide RDX:RAX by `divisor` (`idiv r/m64`, REX.W F7 /7): QUOTIENT → RAX,
	// REMAINDER → RDX. `divisor` is the ONE explicit virtual USE (any GPR the allocator
	// keeps out of RAX/RDX). implicitUses = 0x1 (reads RAX — the dividend low half; it
	// also reads RDX, but that is EXCLUDED from implicitUses because `cqo` — not any
	// tracked value — is what puts RDX's contents there, so RDX's role is captured
	// entirely by implicitDefs). implicitDefs = 0x5
	// (the RAX bit 0x1 OR the RDX bit 0x4 — it WRITES both), which the clobber→forbidden
	// path uses to keep every value live across the idiv, AND the divisor operand itself,
	// out of RAX/RDX. idiv TRAPS (#DE) on divide-by-zero and INT_MIN / -1; the trap is a
	// runtime-fault-handler concern (Workstream-R / M6), so there is no compiler guard.
	// idiv leaves EFLAGS UNDEFINED — a "may clobber", which is why `StdOp.div`/`mod`
	// declare `clobbersFlags: true`. APPENDED at the END of the union, after `cqo`.
	idivReg(divisor TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0x1, implicitDefs: 0x5}
	// Push a physical GPR onto the stack (`push r64`, 50+r, +REX.B for r8–r15). POST-
	// REGALLOC and physical-only: `insertPrologueEpilogue` emits one per callee-saved
	// register the coloring actually used, at the function's entry, so the callee-saved
	// convention holds even though the allocator now colors values into rbx/r12–r15. It
	// carries an `X64Register` directly (never a virtual — it is inserted AFTER coloring,
	// so it is invisible to liveness/coloring, like `xchgRegReg`). Sets no flags.
	// APPENDED at the END of the union, after `idivReg`, so no existing `… to idivReg`
	// range arm can silently swallow it (the band-append invariant — each such arm fails
	// to compile until consciously extended).
	pushReg(reg X64Register) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Pop a physical GPR from the stack (`pop r64`, 58+r, +REX.B), the exact mirror of
	// `pushReg`. `insertPrologueEpilogue` emits one per used callee-saved register before
	// each `ret`, in REVERSE push order. Physical-only, post-regalloc. APPENDED after
	// `pushReg`.
	popReg(reg X64Register) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Spill a register to a stack slot (`mov [rsp + slotDisp], srcReg`, REX.W 89 /r,
	// SIB base=rsp). The cold-spill live-range splitter (SplitLiveRanges, M5.3) inserts
	// it right after a spilled value's def; it USES `srcReg`, so — unlike pushReg/popReg —
	// it carries a VIRTUAL register the allocator colors. `slot` is a dense per-function
	// SpillSlot index the encoder turns into a `[rsp + disp]` displacement. `isMemory`/
	// `isStore` are true (it writes the stack); it sets no flags. APPENDED at the END of
	// the union, after `popReg`, so no existing `… to popReg` range arm can SILENTLY
	// swallow it — each such arm instead fails to compile until consciously extended (the
	// band-append invariant). It is placed after the post-regalloc pushReg/popReg because
	// it is the newest band; its virtual-carrying, pre-regalloc nature is a property of the
	// op, not its position (the invariant is about compile-time exhaustiveness, not phase).
	storeSlotReg(slot SpillSlot, srcReg TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Reload a stack slot into a register (`mov destReg, [rsp + slotDisp]`, REX.W 8B /r,
	// SIB base=rsp), the load mirror of `storeSlotReg`. The splitter inserts it before a
	// spilled value's use, DEFINING a FRESH ValueId (the design's dominating-reload rule):
	// its uses are rewritten to that fresh value, so the program stays SSA and the colorer
	// needs no special handling. It carries a VIRTUAL `destReg` the allocator colors.
	// `isMemory` true, `isStore` FALSE (a pure load); it sets no flags. APPENDED after
	// `storeSlotReg`, at the END of the union.
	loadRegSlot(destReg TargetVReg, slot SpillSlot) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// --- P1.0d.2: the BIT band (specs/bitwise-operators.md) ---
	//
	// APPENDED AT THE END OF THE UNION, all twelve of them, and that placement is the
	// point: EVERY range arm over `TargetOp` in the passes (`movRegReg to idivReg`,
	// `pushReg to loadRegSlot`, `xchgRegReg to iatCall`, …) stops at or before
	// `loadRegSlot`, so an op inserted anywhere ELSE would be SILENTLY SWALLOWED by one
	// of them — misclassified in the operand model, the splitter, the colorer and the
	// prologue pass at once, with no compiler error anywhere. Appending past the last
	// arm instead makes each of those matches fail to compile until it is consciously
	// extended. This is the band-append invariant, and it is the reason this whole
	// band sits here rather than next to the ALU ops it belongs with semantically.
	//
	// `and`/`or`/`xor r/m64, r64` are genuinely two-address on x64 (no 3-operand form),
	// so — exactly like `subRegReg`/`imulRegReg` — `dest` is a REUSE-of-`lhs` def: the
	// allocator gives `dest` `lhs`'s register when `lhs` dies here (no copy), else
	// materializes `mov dest, lhs` before the op. All three write EFLAGS.
	andRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	orRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	xorRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 64-bit bitwise complement (`not dest`, group-3 `F7 /2`) — `dest = not src`, the
	// INTEGER reading of the `not` keyword. Two-address like `negReg` (its `F7 /3`
	// sibling), so `dest` is a REUSE-of-`src` def. UNLIKE `neg`, x86 `NOT` writes NO
	// flags — which is why `StdOp.unaryOp`'s `clobbersFlags: true` is an
	// over-approximation: `bitNot` lands here and clobbers nothing, while `neg` and
	// `logicalNot` (an `xor`) do (see StdOpMeta.clobbersFlags).
	notReg(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// The IMMEDIATE forms of the three word ALU ops (group-1 `81 /ext id`, sign-extended
	// imm32). Produced from a `binOpImm`, so the literal needs no register. Still
	// two-address (`and dest, imm` modifies dest in place) ⇒ REUSE-of-`lhs` def, unlike
	// the `lea`/`imul` immediate forms above, whose destination is independent.
	//
	// `xorRegImm32` carries a SECOND job: it is the LOGICAL `not` (`xor dest, 1`) — a
	// bool is 0/1, so flipping bit 0 negates it. That is why the bool `not` needs no op
	// of its own (see StdToX64Conversion.lowerUnaryOp).
	andRegImm32(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	orRegImm32(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	xorRegImm32(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Shift by a CONSTANT count (group-2 `C1 /4` shl, `C1 /7` sar, `C1 /5` shr).
	// Two-address ⇒ REUSE-of-`lhs`.
	//
	// BOTH right shifts exist, because they are two different instructions and Maxon needs
	// both: `sar` replicates the sign (`StdBinOpcode.shr` — a SIGNED left operand) and `shr`
	// fills with zeros (`StdBinOpcode.shrLogical` — an UNSIGNED one, `int(0 to u64.max)`).
	// The Std tier has already chosen; this tier only encodes.
	shlRegImm8(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, imm ShiftCount) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	sarRegImm8(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, imm ShiftCount) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	shrRegImm8(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, imm ShiftCount) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Shift by a VARIABLE count (`shl`/`sar r/m64, cl` — group-2 `D3 /4` / `D3 /7`). x64
	// has NO register-register shift: the count must be in **CL**, and nowhere else. So
	// the count is a FIXED-REGISTER operand, modelled exactly as `idiv`'s dividend is —
	// StdToX64Conversion pre-moves it with `mov rcx, <count>` and the shift names RCX
	// only IMPLICITLY.
	//
	// implicitUses = 0x2 (the RCX bit — `X64Register.rcx.rawValue` == 1, so 1<<1). That
	// mask is what keeps `lhs` AND `dest` out of RCX: `forbidOperandsFromImplicit` adds
	// `implicitUses ∪ implicitDefs` to the forbidden set of the op's own explicit virtual
	// operands, and the conflict here is SIMULTANEOUS (the shift reads CL in the very
	// instruction that reads/writes dest), which is precisely the condition that helper
	// documents as its correctness precondition. Without it, `dest` could be colored RCX
	// and the allocator's reuse copy (`mov rcx, lhs`) would destroy the shift count
	// before the shift read it. implicitDefs is 0 — a shift does not write RCX.
	// (A union-backing literal cannot reference a named `let`, so the mask is inline.)
	shlRegCl(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0x2, implicitDefs: 0}
	sarRegCl(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0x2, implicitDefs: 0}
	shrRegCl(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0x2, implicitDefs: 0}
	// MATERIALIZE a comparison's boolean into a register (`setcc dest8` + `movzx dest,
	// dest8`) — one TargetOp, two instructions, because the pair is indivisible: `setcc`
	// writes only the LOW BYTE, so the upper 56 bits would keep whatever the register
	// held and the "bool" would read as garbage. Modelling them as two ops would let the
	// allocator colour them apart, which is exactly the thing that must not happen.
	//
	// Emitted ONLY for a `cmp` whose result is a genuine VALUE (`let r = a > b`, a phi
	// operand of a short-circuit merge). A `cmp` that merely feeds the `condBranch`
	// terminating its own block is still FUSED into `cmp` + `jcc` and materializes
	// nothing — see StdToX64Conversion.computeFusedCmps.
	//
	// ONE def, NO uses: it reads EFLAGS, which are not a tracked register. Writes NO flags
	// — `setcc` READS them, the same relationship `jcc` has to them. That is what makes a
	// compare that is BOTH fused and materialized correct: the `setcc` sitting between the
	// `cmp` and the `jcc` cannot disturb what the `jcc` reads.
	setccReg(dest TargetVReg, cond X64CondCode) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// --- P1.0d.5b: the DATA-SECTION band (specs/static-variables.md) ---
	//
	// APPENDED AT THE END OF THE UNION, past `setccReg`, for the reason the BIT band above
	// gives at length: every range arm over `TargetOp` stops at or before the old tail, so an
	// op inserted anywhere else is silently swallowed by one of them. Appending past it makes
	// each of those matches fail to compile until consciously extended.
	//
	// `lea dest, [rip + disp32]` to a `.data` global — the address of `label`'s slot. The
	// disp32 is emitted ZERO and an `RdataRelocation` of kind `dataSectionRipRelDisp32` is
	// recorded; the PE writer patches it once `.data`'s RVA is known. `label` is a name, not a
	// value, so it is a `ByteArray` — the same shape `callDirect`'s `targetName` has, and for
	// the same reason.
	//
	// ONE plain def, NO uses: a RIP-relative `lea` reads no register at all (RIP is not
	// allocatable), which is exactly why `StdOp.globalAddr` can declare `isPure: true` — the
	// op is rematerializable ANYWHERE, with no live input to keep alive to do it. `lea` sets
	// no flags.
	leaRegGlobal(dest TargetVReg, label ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// `mov dest, [base + disp]` (word64) or `movzx dest, byte [base + disp]` (byte) — load,
	// zero-extending a byte to the full 64-bit register so a bool reads as a clean 0/1.
	//
	// `dest` and `base` are INDEPENDENT (no reuse): the destination of a load need not be its
	// address register, so no seed copy is ever emitted. `isMemory: true`, `isStore: false` —
	// a pure load, which the scheduler may not move across a store. Neither encoding writes
	// flags.
	//
	// The width rides an OPCODE FIELD rather than splitting the variant, per the dialect's own
	// rule: the byte and word64 forms share this exact operand shape AND this exact metadata,
	// and differ only in which encoder runs.
	loadRegBaseDisp(dest TargetVReg, base TargetVReg, disp Imm32, width X64AccessWidth) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// `mov [base + disp], src` — store, the mirror of `loadRegBaseDisp`. TWO uses and no def.
	// `isStore: true`. APPENDED at the END of the union, after `loadRegBaseDisp`.
	storeBaseDispReg(base TargetVReg, disp Imm32, src TargetVReg, width X64AccessWidth) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// --- P1.0d.4: the SSE band (specs-shv2/float-type.md, specs-shv2/float-compare-branch.md) ---
	//
	// APPENDED AT THE END OF THE UNION, after `storeBaseDispReg`, per the band-append
	// invariant documented on the bit band above — every range arm over `TargetOp` stops at
	// or before `loadRegSlot`, so anything inserted elsewhere is SILENTLY swallowed.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ THIS BAND IS SMALL FOR A REASON, AND THE REASON IS THE ONE-ENUM DESIGN.
	// There is no `movsdRegReg`, no float spill, no float reload, and no float `xchg` here —
	// because a MOVE, a SPILL, a RELOAD and a SWAP are class-agnostic CONCEPTS. v1 mints a
	// second register enum and therefore needs a parallel op for every one of them. shv2 puts
	// both files in ONE enum, so `movRegReg` / `storeSlotReg` / `loadRegSlot` / `xchgRegReg`
	// already say everything those ops need to say, and the ENCODER picks `mov` vs `movsd`
	// (and `xchg` vs a triple-`xorpd`) from `regClassOf` of the RESOLVED register.
	// ⇒ **CLASS-AGNOSTIC OPS, CLASS-DISPATCHED ENCODERS.** That one rule is why SSA
	// destruction needs no float case, the splitter needs no float spill op, and the
	// allocator's spill/reload machinery is untouched by this rung. A spill slot is 8 bytes
	// and an f64 is 8 bytes, so even the frame layout is unchanged.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ AND HERE IS WHAT THAT DESIGN COSTS — A NEW SILENT-WRONG-ANSWER SITE.
	// `resolvePhysical` returns an `X64Register` and asks no questions. `storeSlotReg` encodes
	// `mov [rsp+disp], src` from it — hand that encoder `xmm3` (rawValue 19) and the low
	// nibble is 3, so it assembles **`mov [rsp+disp], rbx`**: a GPR spill of a register nobody
	// named, with no error at any layer. The one-enum trade buys uniformity everywhere above
	// the encoder and pays for it in exactly this spot, where a bare rawValue can mean either
	// file. ⇒ **A GPR-ONLY ENCODER MUST RESOLVE THROUGH A CLASS-CHECKING ACCESSOR that panics
	// on an XMM (and vice versa), and a class-agnostic op MUST dispatch on `regClassOf`.**
	// Never hand a raw `resolvePhysical` result to a fixed-class encoder.
	//
	// The four ALU ops are genuinely two-address on SSE (`addsd xmm1, xmm2/m64` — no
	// 3-operand form without AVX), so — exactly like `subRegReg` — `dest` is a REUSE-of-`lhs`
	// def: the allocator gives `dest` `lhs`'s register when `lhs` dies here, else materializes
	// the copy first. ⚠ NONE of them writes EFLAGS — which is why `StdOp.binOp`'s
	// `clobbersFlags: true` is an over-approximation for floats (see StdOpMeta.clobbersFlags).
	addsdRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	subsdRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	mulsdRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Float division. Its Std source is `binOp(div)`, NOT `StdOp.div`: `idiv` faults and
	// `divsd` does not (IEEE answers inf/nan), so float division is pure and rides the
	// non-trapping band. See StdBinOpcode.
	divsdRegReg(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// The float compare. TWO uses, NO def — it sets flags only, exactly like `cmpRegReg`.
	// ⚠ It writes ZF/PF/CF and leaves OF=SF=AF=0, so its branch is the UNSIGNED `ja`/`jae`
	// family, never the signed `jl`/`jg` one. `<` and `<=` are lowered by SWAPPING the
	// operands into that already-NaN-correct family; only `==`/`!=` need the parity bit, and
	// they get it as a second BLOCK, never a second conditional jump in one block. See
	// X64CondCode and specs-shv2/float-compare-branch.md.
	ucomisdRegReg(lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// `movsd dest, [rip + disp32]` — load an f64 constant from `.rdata`. The float twin of
	// `leaRegGlobal`, and it shares that op's every property: ONE plain def, NO uses, reading
	// no allocatable register at all (RIP is not allocatable).
	//
	// ⚠ IT IS **NOT** A CONST MATERIALIZATION, and it inherits `leaRegGlobal`'s reasoning
	// verbatim — read that comment in `opIsConstMaterialization`. It reads no register and its
	// value cannot change, so it IS rematerializable *in principle*. But that predicate asks a
	// NARROWER question: *"can this def be rebuilt by `constValueOfDef` + a `movRegImm`?"* An
	// f64 constant lives behind a RIP-relative RELOCATION into `.rdata`, so rebuilding it means
	// re-emitting the `movsd` AND a fresh relocation — which the remat path has no way to
	// express, and there is no `movsd xmm, imm64` to fall back on. Answering `true` would send
	// it to `constValueOfDef`, which would PANIC.
	// ⇒ It is a real missed optimization, and it is the SAME one `leaRegGlobal` already prices:
	// taking either means generalizing remat from "a constant value" to "an op with no register
	// inputs". Not this rung's. Recorded here rather than silently ranged over.
	movsdRegRip(dest TargetVReg, label ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// ⚠⚠ THE ONLY TWO CROSS-CLASS OPS IN THE DIALECT — they read one file and write the other.
	// `cvtsi2sd xmm, r64` (int → f64, `float-promotion`'s widening) and `cvttsd2si r64, xmm`
	// (f64 → int, TRUNCATING toward zero — the `trunc()` builtin, and the `t` in its mnemonic
	// is the whole reason it is the right instruction for a spec that says `3.99` gives `3`).
	// Both are PLAIN defs (dest independent of src), so no seed copy is emitted.
	//
	// They need NO special class machinery: a value's class comes from its Std type, and these
	// ops sit exactly where the type changes — `cvtsi2sd`'s operand IS an i64 value (gpr) and
	// its result IS an f64 value (xmm). The operand model states the two registers; the class
	// column answers each from its own value. This is what the class-per-value design buys.
	cvtsi2sdRegReg(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	cvttsd2siRegReg(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// `lea dest, [rip + disp32]` to an `.rdata` constant — the address of a String literal's 48-byte
	// record (P1.2 wave A). The `.rdata` twin of `leaRegGlobal` (which targets `.data`), and it shares
	// that op's every property: ONE plain def, NO uses, reads no allocatable register at all (RIP is
	// not allocatable), sets no flags. The disp32 is emitted ZERO and an `RdataRelocation` of kind
	// `ripRelDisp32` is recorded; the PE writer patches it once `.rdata`'s RVA is known. `label` is a
	// name, not a value, so it is a `ByteArray` — the same shape `leaRegGlobal`'s carries.
	//
	// APPENDED AT THE END OF THE UNION, past `cvttsd2siRegReg`, per the band-append invariant: every
	// `andRegReg to cvttsd2siRegReg` range arm (which covers `leaRegGlobal` today) now fails to compile
	// until it is consciously extended to `andRegReg to leaRegRdata`, exactly where `leaRegGlobal`
	// already sits inside it.
	leaRegRdata(dest TargetVReg, label ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// --- arm64-macOS SCALAR-CORE band (USER RULING 2026-07-17) ---
	//
	// APPENDED AT THE END OF THE UNION, one contiguous band, per the band-append invariant every
	// preceding band documents: every range arm over `TargetOp` in the x64/shared passes stops at or
	// before `leaRegRdata`, so an arm64 op inserted anywhere else would be SILENTLY swallowed and
	// misclassified. Appending past the last arm makes each of those matches fail to compile until it
	// is consciously extended with an `arm64Ret to arm64Syscall` arm.
	//
	// One allocator, one op union (USER RULING): these arms carry the NEUTRAL `TargetVReg` and the
	// neutral `RegNum`, exactly as the x64 arms do — the arm64 ENCODER (wave 1) maps a resolved
	// `RegNum` to `Arm64Register`. arm64 is a 3-ADDRESS ISA, so its ALU ops take three INDEPENDENT
	// operands (plain defs, NO two-address `Reuse` — cleaner than x64). Reg-reg moves, the
	// spill/reload SLOT ops and base+disp loads/stores are CLASS- and ISA-AGNOSTIC and are REUSED
	// from the shared band (`movRegReg` / `storeSlotReg` / `loadRegSlot` / `loadRegBaseDisp` /
	// `storeBaseDispReg`), so they do not reappear here — the arm64 encoder gives `movRegReg` a
	// `mov`/`fmov`, `storeSlotReg` a `str`, and so on.
	//
	// ⭐ NOW EXERCISED. The arm64 isel produces these ops and the full pipeline runs on arm64-macOS
	// (the scalar-core backend + M2 runtime floor): the operand model (TargetOperands), the printer
	// (TargetPrinter), and the allocator's rewrite/hint/self-move passes all carry real arm64 arms
	// (see `colorTargetOp`) — no longer the range-PANICs the wave-1 stub left before arm64 ran the
	// allocator.

	// `ret` — return to caller. implicitUses = 0x1 (the x0 bit): arm64 returns its primary value in
	// x0, which is what lets the allocator admit x0 into the pool (nothing is live across
	// `mov x0, retval; ret`). The target-dispatched counterpart of the x64 `ret`'s R8 (0x100).
	arm64Ret = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0x1, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Unconditional branch `b` to a function-local block.
	arm64Branch(targetBlockId BlockId) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Conditional branch `b.cond` — taken when the flags a preceding `arm64Cmp`/`arm64Fcmp` set
	// satisfy `cond`. Reads flags, writes none.
	arm64CondBranch(cond Arm64CondCode, targetBlockId BlockId) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// `cbz` / `cbnz` — branch on a register being zero / non-zero, WITHOUT a preceding compare (the
	// fused zero-test arm64 has and x64 does not). One register USE, no flags.
	arm64Cbz(reg TargetVReg, targetBlockId BlockId) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Cbnz(reg TargetVReg, targetBlockId BlockId) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Materialize a 64-bit immediate — the encoder emits the `movz`/`movk` ladder (or the 32-bit
	// `movn` fast path). One plain def.
	arm64MovImm(dest TargetVReg, value MachineWord) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Frame setup / teardown — the encoder expands each to the arm64 frame (stp x29/x30, mov x29,
	// sp, sub sp / the mirror). Carries its own `stackSize`, like the x64 pair.
	arm64Prologue(stackSize StackSize) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Epilogue(stackSize StackSize) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 3-address integer ALU — `dest`, `lhs`, `rhs` are three INDEPENDENT operands (arm64 has no
	// two-address form), so `dest` is a PLAIN def and no reuse copy is ever needed.
	arm64Add(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Sub(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Mul(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Signed / unsigned divide. ⚠ arm64 `sdiv`/`udiv` do NOT trap on divide-by-zero (they return 0),
	// unlike x64 `idiv` (#DE) — the isel emits an explicit `cmp rhs,#0; b.ne; bl panic` guard (a
	// KNOWN DIVERGENCE, M1 design notes). Both are plain 3-address defs.
	arm64Sdiv(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Udiv(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Remainder as `msub`: `dest = minuend - mulLhs * mulRhs`. The isel builds `rem = x - (x/y)*y`
	// with `mulLhs` = the quotient, `mulRhs` = the divisor, `minuend` = the dividend — one def, three
	// independent uses.
	arm64Msub(dest TargetVReg, mulLhs TargetVReg, mulRhs TargetVReg, minuend TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 3-address bitwise + variable shifts (`lslv`/`asrv`/`lsrv` — arm64's shift-by-register takes an
	// ordinary GPR count, NOT a fixed register like x64's CL, so no implicit register is pinned).
	arm64And(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Orr(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Eor(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Lslv(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Asrv(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Lsrv(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// 2-address negate / bitwise-NOT (`neg` = `sub xd,xzr,xn`; `mvn` = `orn xd,xzr,xn`) — `dest` is
	// INDEPENDENT of `src` (arm64 reads the zero register as the other operand), so a PLAIN def.
	arm64Neg(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Mvn(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// The IMMEDIATE forms of the ALU ops (`add`/`sub` #imm12, the `and`/`orr`/`eor` bitmask-immediate
	// forms, and the constant shifts `lsl`/`asr`/`lsr` #imm). arm64's are 3-address — `dest` is
	// INDEPENDENT of `src` — so, UNLIKE x64's two-address immediate forms, these are PLAIN defs.
	arm64AddImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64SubImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64AndImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64OrrImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64EorImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64LslImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm ShiftCount) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64AsrImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm ShiftCount) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64LsrImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm ShiftCount) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Compare (register / immediate) — two-use / one-use, sets NZCV, defines no register.
	arm64Cmp(lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64CmpImm(reg TargetVReg, imm Imm32) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Materialize a condition into a register (`cset xd, cond` = `csinc xd,xzr,xzr,invert(cond)`).
	// One def, reads flags — the arm64 twin of `setccReg`.
	arm64Cset(dest TargetVReg, cond Arm64CondCode) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// PIE address materialization via the ADRP+ADD page/page-off pair. Three label kinds, three
	// relocation targets: a function's `.text` (`leaFuncAddr`), an `.rdata` constant record
	// (`leaRdata`), a `.data` global (`leaGlobal`). Each is one plain def reading no register.
	arm64LeaFuncAddr(dest TargetVReg, funcName ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64LeaRdata(dest TargetVReg, label ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64LeaGlobal(dest TargetVReg, label ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// Direct / indirect call (`bl` / `blr`). Full call-barrier semantics: implicitDefs =
	// 0x7FFF807F8000FFFF — every CALLER-saved register of both files the AAPCS64-derived ABI
	// clobbers: GPRs x0..x15 (bits 0..15 = 0xFFFF), SIMD d0..d7 (bits 31..38) and d16..d31 (bits
	// 47..62); x19..x28 and d8..d15 are callee-saved and DELIBERATELY excluded. This equals
	// `RegisterAllocator.callerSavedMask` for arm64 and `assertCallClobberConsistent` holds them
	// equal. A union-backing literal cannot reference a named `let`, so the mask is inline.
	arm64BranchLink(targetName ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0x7FFF807F8000FFFF}
	arm64BranchLinkReg(reg TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0x7FFF807F8000FFFF}
	// Float ALU / compare / int↔float conversion. The four ALU ops are 3-address PLAIN defs (arm64
	// FP has a real 3-operand form, so no reuse — cleaner than x64's two-address SSE). `fcmp` sets
	// NZCV and defines nothing. `scvtf` reads a GPR and writes an FP reg; `fcvtzs` the reverse
	// (TRUNCATING toward zero) — their classes come from each VALUE's Std type, as with x64's
	// cvtsi2sd/cvttsd2si. The FP reg-reg move is the shared `movRegReg` (encoded `fmov`), so it does
	// not appear here.
	arm64Fadd(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Fsub(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Fmul(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Fdiv(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Fcmp(lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Scvtf(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	arm64Fcvtzs(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// `ldr d, [pc-rel rdata]` — load an f64 constant from `.rdata` via the ADRP+LDR pair. One plain
	// def reading no allocatable register; the float twin of `leaRdata`. isMemory (a load).
	arm64FpLoadRdata(dest TargetVReg, label ByteArray) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: false, isCall: false, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0}
	// A GOT-indirect libSystem import call (`adrp x16, GOTPAGE; ldr x16, [x16, GOTOFF]; blr x16`) — the
	// macOS twin of x64's `iatCall`, used by the arm64 isel to lower `osAllocPages`→`mmap` and
	// `osWriteStdout`→`write` (the M2 runtime floor). APPENDED at the END of the arm64 band (band-append
	// invariant), so every `… to arm64FpLoadRdata` range arm fails to compile until consciously extended.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ IT CANNOT REUSE `iatCall`, AND THE REASON IS THE CLOBBER. `iatCall`'s single `TargetOpMeta`
	// carries the x64 caller-saved mask (0xFFFF0FC7), whose bits name x64 `RegNum`s — a DIFFERENT numbering
	// from arm64's. Routing an arm64 mmap/write through `iatCall` would forbid a value live across it the
	// x64 set (leaving x3/x4/x5/x12–x15, all arm64 caller-saved, free to colour and be clobbered): a silent
	// miscompile, and `__slab_alloc` genuinely holds values live across its page call (the exact shape the
	// `iatCall` comment records for `VirtualAlloc`). So this op carries the ARM64 caller-saved clobber:
	// implicitDefs = 0x7FFF807F8000FFFF — GPRs x0..x15 (0xFFFF), SIMD d0..d7 (bits 31..38) and d16..d31
	// (bits 47..62); x19..x28/d8..d15 are callee-saved and excluded. Identical to `arm64BranchLink`, and
	// `assertArm64CallClobbers` holds it equal to `arm64CallerSavedMask`. A union-backing literal cannot
	// reference a named `let`, so the mask is inline (guarded there). `slotIndex` names the libSystem GOT
	// slot the Mach-O writer patches.
	arm64ImportCall(slotIndex RelocSlotIndex) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0x7FFF807F8000FFFF}
	// A raw Linux `svc #0` supervisor call (`movz x8, #number; svc #0`) — the LINUX sibling of
	// `arm64ImportCall`, and the reason arm64-linux needs no import table, no GOT and no dynamic
	// linker at all: macOS FORBIDS raw syscalls and forces every OS primitive through libSystem,
	// Linux permits them. `osExit`/`osWriteStdout`/`osAllocPages` lower to this on Linux and to
	// `arm64ImportCall` on macOS (`Arm64Runtime.arm64OsCallKind` is the one door that decides).
	//
	// ⭐ IT IS AN ORDINARY CALL, NOT A SECOND ABI. v1 modelled its syscall as a SLOT-based op
	// carrying its own operand array (`syscall(resultSlot, number, argSlots)`), because v1's
	// backend is slot-based — its operands live at `[x29, #16+i*8]` and are invisible to coloring.
	// shv2 is SSA/vreg with a real allocator, so bolting a parallel slot path onto it would be a
	// second, uncoloured argument convention. Instead the LOWERING moves arguments into physical
	// x0..x5 and reads the result out of x0 exactly as `lowerCall` does — the same machinery, the
	// same liveness argument — and this op carries only the syscall NUMBER (materialised into x8
	// by the encoder, where the AArch64 Linux ABI wants it).
	//
	// The meta is `arm64BranchLink`'s, byte for byte, and that is what makes the above sound:
	// `isCall: true` plus implicitDefs = 0x7FFF807F8000FFFF (GPRs x0..x15, SIMD d0..d7 and
	// d16..d31 — every AAPCS64 caller-saved register; x19..x28/d8..d15 excluded) confines any
	// value live ACROSS the syscall to the callee-saved half. x8 is inside that mask, so the
	// encoder is free to clobber it. v1 additionally declared `implicitGprDefs: 1`, which is
	// redundant once the call clobber covers x0. A union-backing literal cannot reference a named
	// `let`, so the mask is inline (`assertArm64CallClobbers` holds it equal to
	// `arm64CallerSavedMask`).
	arm64Syscall(number MachineWord) = TargetOpMeta{isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, implicitUses: 0, implicitDefs: 0x7FFF807F8000FFFF}
end 'TargetOp'

// The memory-access widths the `.data` load/store encoders have. TWO, because two are what the
// language can put in a global today: an `int` (`i64`, 8 bytes) and a `bool` (`i1`, 1 byte).
//
// A TARGET-tier enum rather than reusing `StdType`, and the narrowness is the point: it is the
// exact domain `encodeLoadRegBaseDisp`/`encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp` cover, so the conversion from
// a Std type is a CHECKED decision at one door (`x64AccessWidthFor`) that names what it cannot yet
// encode. Passing a `StdType` straight through would let an `i16` or an `f64` reach an encoder with
// no form for it and emit a silently wrong-width access. It grows a case per width the encoders
// grow a form for.
export enum X64AccessWidth
	byte
	word64
end 'X64AccessWidth'

export typealias TargetOpArray = Array with TargetOp

// TargetModule — the Target-dialect instantiation of the generic IrModule
// container; holds a whole program's Target-tier ops/blocks/functions. Unlike
// v1's bespoke TargetModule struct (which carried BOTH an x64 and an arm64 op
// array behind a `cpu` discriminator), shv2 makes the Target tier a plain
// `IrModule with TargetOp` — consistent with MaxonModule/StdModule —
// so the shared IrModule machinery (appendOp/setTerminator/compactOps) is
// reused with no bespoke duplicate. arm64 grows as additional `TargetOp` arms
// (or its own dialect) at the arm64 milestone.
export typealias TargetModule = IrModule with TargetOp
